1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is PIXIU,</font>

2
00:00:04,013 --> 00:00:07,447
<font color="#FFFFFF">a comprehensive open-source framework for financial
artificial intelligence,</font>

3
00:00:07,527 --> 00:00:10,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">published at NeurIPS 2023.</font>

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:14,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">Financial language is highly technical,</font>

5
00:00:14,553 --> 00:00:17,987
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet before PIXIU there were no openly released
financial large language models,</font>

6
00:00:18,067 --> 00:00:21,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">no financial instruction tuning data, and no
holistic evaluation benchmark.</font>

7
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:25,013
<font color="#FFFFFF">PIXIU fills all three gaps at once.</font>

8
00:00:25,093 --> 00:00:28,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">It contributes FIT, the first multi-task,</font>

9
00:00:28,607 --> 00:00:32,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">multi-modal financial instruction dataset with one
hundred and thirty-six thousand samples;</font>

10
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:35,553
<font color="#FFFFFF">FinMA,</font>

11
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:39,067
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first openly released instruction-following
financial large language model built by fine-tuning LLaMA;</font>

12
00:00:39,147 --> 00:00:42,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">and FLARE,</font>

13
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:46,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">a standardized benchmark spanning financial natural
language tasks and stock movement prediction.</font>

14
00:00:46,173 --> 00:00:49,607
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together they let the community build, compare,</font>

15
00:00:49,687 --> 00:00:53,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">and advance financial LLMs in the open.</font>

16
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:57,487
<font color="#FFFFFF">Financial technology has advanced rapidly with NLP,</font>

17
00:00:57,567 --> 00:01:01,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the highly technical nature of financial text
demands domain-specific models.</font>

18
00:01:01,635 --> 00:01:05,622
<font color="#FFFFFF">Existing financial pre-trained models like finBERT
and FLANG are small,</font>

19
00:01:05,702 --> 00:01:09,689
<font color="#FFFFFF">below one billion parameters, limiting their
generalization.</font>

20
00:01:09,769 --> 00:01:13,756
<font color="#FFFFFF">The one large financial model, the
fifty-billion-parameter BloombergGPT,</font>

21
00:01:13,836 --> 00:01:17,824
<font color="#FFFFFF">is proprietary: neither its weights nor its
training data are released,</font>

22
00:01:17,904 --> 00:01:21,891
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it is not instruction-following.</font>

23
00:01:21,971 --> 00:01:25,958
<font color="#FFFFFF">Critically,</font>

24
00:01:26,038 --> 00:01:30,025
<font color="#FFFFFF">there are no open financial instruction datasets
and no standardized benchmarks for comprehensively</font>

25
00:01:30,105 --> 00:01:34,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">assessing financial LLMs. This leaves the research
community without the resources needed</font>

26
00:01:34,173 --> 00:01:38,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">to push financial AI forward in the open.</font>

27
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:42,518
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two gaps motivate this work.</font>

28
00:01:42,598 --> 00:01:46,576
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

29
00:01:46,656 --> 00:01:50,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">instruction tuning has proven essential for
improving a model's zero-shot ability on downstream tasks,</font>

30
00:01:50,714 --> 00:01:54,692
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet no financial instruction data exists to enable it.</font>

31
00:01:54,772 --> 00:01:58,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

32
00:01:58,830 --> 00:02:02,808
<font color="#FFFFFF">existing financial benchmarks such as FLUE cover
only natural language processing tasks.</font>

33
00:02:02,888 --> 00:02:06,866
<font color="#FFFFFF">They ignore financial prediction tasks like stock
movement prediction,</font>

34
00:02:06,946 --> 00:02:10,924
<font color="#FFFFFF">which require exploiting both text and time-series
data and are far more aligned with real-world financial scenarios.</font>

35
00:02:11,004 --> 00:02:14,982
<font color="#FFFFFF">PIXIU is built to close both gaps with open
resources, multi-task coverage,</font>

36
00:02:15,062 --> 00:02:19,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">multi-modal data, and greater task diversity.</font>

37
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:21,502
<font color="#FFFFFF">PIXIU makes four contributions.</font>

38
00:02:21,582 --> 00:02:23,246
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it introduces FIT,</font>

39
00:02:23,326 --> 00:02:28,546
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first multi-task and multi-modal instruction
tuning dataset for finance,</font>

40
00:02:28,626 --> 00:02:34,335
<font color="#FFFFFF">covering five tasks and nine datasets with one
hundred thirty-six thousand samples.</font>

41
00:02:34,415 --> 00:02:36,287
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it introduces FLARE,</font>

42
00:02:36,367 --> 00:02:43,819
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first evaluation benchmark that includes both
financial language understanding and financial prediction.</font>

43
00:02:43,899 --> 00:02:47,586
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it introduces FinMA, the first openly released,</font>

44
00:02:47,666 --> 00:02:51,282
<font color="#FFFFFF">instruction-following financial large language model,</font>

45
00:02:51,362 --> 00:02:56,861
<font color="#FFFFFF">achieving state-of-the-art on three financial NLP
tasks and one prediction task.</font>

46
00:02:56,941 --> 00:03:00,348
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fourth, it benchmarks FinMA against existing LLMs,</font>

47
00:03:00,428 --> 00:03:05,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">revealing both their superiority and their key
limitations for finance.</font>

48
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:09,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method has three stages.</font>

49
00:03:09,619 --> 00:03:13,479
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

50
00:03:13,559 --> 00:03:17,418
<font color="#FFFFFF">PIXIU gathers open-released data across five
financial tasks: sentiment analysis,</font>

51
00:03:17,498 --> 00:03:21,357
<font color="#FFFFFF">news headline classification, named entity
recognition, question answering,</font>

52
00:03:21,437 --> 00:03:25,296
<font color="#FFFFFF">and stock movement prediction.</font>

53
00:03:25,376 --> 00:03:29,236
<font color="#FFFFFF">Domain experts write diverse task-specific
instructions for each task,</font>

54
00:03:29,316 --> 00:03:33,175
<font color="#FFFFFF">which are assembled with the data samples to form
the FIT instruction dataset.</font>

55
00:03:33,255 --> 00:03:37,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">This data is multi-modal, spanning text, tables
from financial reports,</font>

56
00:03:37,194 --> 00:03:41,054
<font color="#FFFFFF">and historical stock prices as time series.</font>

57
00:03:41,134 --> 00:03:44,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

58
00:03:45,073 --> 00:03:48,932
<font color="#FFFFFF">LLaMA checkpoints at seven and thirty billion
parameters are fine-tuned on FIT with multi-task instruction tuning,</font>

59
00:03:49,012 --> 00:03:52,871
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing the FinMA model family.</font>

60
00:03:52,951 --> 00:03:56,811
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, FinMA and other LLMs are evaluated on the
FLARE benchmark,</font>

61
00:03:56,891 --> 00:04:00,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">which unifies four financial NLP tasks with six
datasets and one prediction task with three datasets.</font>

62
00:04:01,130 --> 00:04:04,467
<font color="#FFFFFF">FIT, the financial instruction tuning dataset,</font>

63
00:04:04,547 --> 00:04:11,672
<font color="#FFFFFF">contains one hundred thirty-six thousand
instruction samples across five tasks and nine datasets.</font>

64
00:04:11,752 --> 00:04:18,209
<font color="#FFFFFF">The FLARE evaluation benchmark covers four
financial NLP tasks with six datasets and one</font>

65
00:04:18,289 --> 00:04:21,626
<font color="#FFFFFF">financial prediction task with three datasets.</font>

66
00:04:21,706 --> 00:04:26,232
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sentiment analysis uses the Financial Phrase Bank
and FiQA-SA,</font>

67
00:04:26,312 --> 00:04:30,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">news headline classification uses the Headline dataset,</font>

68
00:04:30,397 --> 00:04:34,328
<font color="#FFFFFF">named entity recognition uses a financial NER dataset,</font>

69
00:04:34,408 --> 00:04:37,597
<font color="#FFFFFF">question answering uses FinQA and ConvFinQA,</font>

70
00:04:37,677 --> 00:04:42,351
<font color="#FFFFFF">and stock movement prediction uses BigData22,
ACL18, and CIKM18.</font>

71
00:04:42,431 --> 00:04:48,516
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each task is scored with its standard metric, such
as weighted F1, entity-level F1,</font>

72
00:04:48,596 --> 00:04:54,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">exact-match accuracy, and the Matthews correlation
coefficient for prediction.</font>

73
00:04:54,690 --> 00:04:59,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the FLARE benchmark,</font>

74
00:04:59,315 --> 00:05:03,861
<font color="#FFFFFF">the fine-tuned FinMA models significantly
outperform other large language models on most financial NLP tasks,</font>

75
00:05:03,941 --> 00:05:08,486
<font color="#FFFFFF">including sentiment analysis, headline
classification, and named entity recognition.</font>

76
00:05:08,566 --> 00:05:13,112
<font color="#FFFFFF">For example, on the Financial Phrase Bank sentiment
dataset,</font>

77
00:05:13,192 --> 00:05:17,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">FinMA-30B outperforms GPT-4 by ten percent F1 and
BloombergGPT by thirty-seven percent F1.</font>

78
00:05:17,817 --> 00:05:22,363
<font color="#FFFFFF">This demonstrates the value of tailoring LLMs to
the financial domain through instruction tuning.</font>

79
00:05:22,443 --> 00:05:26,988
<font color="#FFFFFF">However,</font>

80
00:05:27,068 --> 00:05:31,614
<font color="#FFFFFF">FinMA underperforms on question answering tasks
that demand quantitative reasoning,</font>

81
00:05:31,694 --> 00:05:36,239
<font color="#FFFFFF">a limitation inherited from LLaMA's weak
mathematical ability.</font>

82
00:05:36,319 --> 00:05:40,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">And across all models, stock movement prediction
remains challenging,</font>

83
00:05:40,945 --> 00:05:45,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">leaving clear room for future improvement.</font>

84
00:05:45,870 --> 00:05:48,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">Comparing model variants is revealing.</font>

85
00:05:48,490 --> 00:05:56,406
<font color="#FFFFFF">FinMA-30B shows no significantly better performance
than FinMA-7B on most NLP tasks or on stock movement prediction,</font>

86
00:05:56,486 --> 00:06:03,644
<font color="#FFFFFF">indicating that the quality and diversity of the
instruction data matter more than sheer parameter count.</font>

87
00:06:03,724 --> 00:06:08,745
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scale does help on complex quantitative question
answering like ConvFinQA,</font>

88
00:06:08,825 --> 00:06:12,606
<font color="#FFFFFF">mirroring LLaMA's improved math ability at larger sizes,</font>

89
00:06:12,686 --> 00:06:14,605
<font color="#FFFFFF">though it still trails GPT-4.</font>

90
00:06:14,685 --> 00:06:19,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">Notably, FinMA-7B-full, fine-tuned on both NLP and
prediction tasks,</font>

91
00:06:19,372 --> 00:06:25,152
<font color="#FFFFFF">achieves the best performance among all models on
the ACL18 stock prediction dataset,</font>

92
00:06:25,232 --> 00:06:31,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">highlighting the promise of task-specific
instruction tuning for financial prediction.</font>

93
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:34,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture PIXIU's impact.</font>

94
00:06:34,127 --> 00:06:41,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">The FIT instruction dataset holds one hundred
thirty-six thousand samples across five tasks and nine datasets.</font>

95
00:06:42,057 --> 00:06:47,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">FinMA is built on the open LLaMA model at seven and
thirty billion parameters.</font>

96
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:50,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the Financial Phrase Bank sentiment task,</font>

97
00:06:50,852 --> 00:06:56,971
<font color="#FFFFFF">FinMA-30B exceeds GPT-4 by ten percent F1 and
BloombergGPT by thirty-seven percent F1,</font>

98
00:06:57,051 --> 00:07:00,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaching a weighted F1 around zero point eight eight.</font>

99
00:07:00,872 --> 00:07:07,857
<font color="#FFFFFF">FinMA achieves state-of-the-art on three financial
NLP tasks and one financial prediction dataset,</font>

100
00:07:07,937 --> 00:07:10,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">all while being fully open-sourced.</font>

101
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:12,153
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

102
00:07:12,233 --> 00:07:18,493
<font color="#FFFFFF">PIXIU provides the financial AI community with its
first fully open triad: an instruction-following</font>

103
00:07:18,573 --> 00:07:20,478
<font color="#FFFFFF">financial large language model,</font>

104
00:07:20,558 --> 00:07:25,345
<font color="#FFFFFF">a large multi-task instruction dataset, and a
holistic evaluation benchmark.</font>

105
00:07:25,425 --> 00:07:30,789
<font color="#FFFFFF">It shows that careful domain instruction tuning can
let a relatively small open model</font>

106
00:07:30,869 --> 00:07:35,143
<font color="#FFFFFF">beat far larger general-purpose systems on
financial language tasks,</font>

107
00:07:35,223 --> 00:07:42,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">while honestly exposing quantitative reasoning and
stock movement prediction as the field's remaining open challenges.</font>
